WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.233
Support vector machines.

2
00:00:02.533 --> 00:00:04.642
Not just any separating boundary.

3
00:00:04.642 --> 00:00:05.600
The widest one.

4
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.286
Many lines can separate two classes.

5
00:00:09.286 --> 00:00:12.600
An SVM asks for the one with the maximum margin.

6
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.900
The margin is the distance from the boundary to the nearest training points of either class.

7
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.533
The dashed lines mark the margin edges.

8
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:23.517
The points touching them are the support vectors.

9
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:28.500
They alone define the boundary, and every other point could be deleted without changing it.

10
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:30.847
Why does it matter?

11
00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:34.833
Fat margins generalize better than boundaries that graze the data.

12
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:36.867
Only the support vectors matter.

13
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.733
A boundary hugging the training points is fragile.

14
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:43.273
For curved data, a straight line fails.

15
00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:44.833
The kernel trick fixes it.

16
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:47.867
Start with data no straight line can separate.

17
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:53.467
Implicitly map the points into a higher dimensional space, without ever computing that mapping.

18
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:55.867
There, a linear boundary separates them.

19
00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:59.723
Back in the original space, it appears as a smooth curve.

20
00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:02.967
The RBF kernel does this with a similarity function.

21
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:06.400
Let's test it on two interleaved moons.

22
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.600
We generate the moons with some noise.

23
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:14.933
Each model scales the features first, because SVMs are distance based, then fits an SVC.

24
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:18.679
The linear kernel scores about zero point eight eight.

25
00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:21.933
The RBF kernel reaches about zero point nine seven.

26
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.533
Now a real scenario.

27
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:33.971
Plot exoplanets by log period and radius, and hot Jupiters and rocky planets form two curved lobes that no straight line separates.

28
00:01:33.971 --> 00:01:36.900
A linear SVC scores zero point eight four.

29
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.222
The RBF kernel wraps a smooth boundary around each lobe and hits zero point nine five.

30
00:01:43.222 --> 00:01:45.532
Same margin maximizing machinery.

31
00:01:45.532 --> 00:01:48.333
The kernel just changed what line means.

32
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:50.454
Let's recap.

33
00:01:50.454 --> 00:01:53.600
An SVM finds the maximum margin boundary.

34
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.867
Support vectors alone define it.

35
00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:58.100
The kernel trick bends the boundary.

36
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.941
And always scale your features first.

37
00:02:00.941 --> 00:02:01.833
Next up: KNN.
